// aeon-os (SMP): real multi-core scheduling, controlled entirely from
// bl0jv2. Builds on boot.bl0's single-threaded cooperative scheduler - the
// VM now provides genuine parallel execution (real worker threads, one per
// "core") via dispatch(fn, core, arg), but the scheduling POLICY (which
// task runs where, when) still lives here, not in the VM: dispatch() is
// pure mechanism, nothing more.
//
// The run queue is protected by BOTH disableInterrupts()/enableInterrupts()
// (interrupt-handler reentrancy on whichever core is mutating it - see
// boot.bl0) AND a real Mutex (races BETWEEN cores, which disableInterrupts()
// does nothing for - see Bl0jMutex's own doc). Both matter here for real:
// with genuine parallelism, two cores really can be inside addTask() at
// the same instant. Scheduler.tasks is a compound structure (an array),
// which is exactly what a Mutex is for - a single raw-memory word has a
// cheaper option instead (see the heap allocator and completion counter
// below), which is why not everything in this file uses a Mutex.
//
// --- atomics instead of a Mutex, where the shared state fits in one word ---
// The kernel heap's own bump pointer and the task-completion counter are
// both single 32-bit words in raw memory, so both use atomicAdd() instead
// of a Mutex + a managed static field - lock-free, and genuinely simpler
// code (kalloc() is one line). A Mutex still protects Scheduler.tasks
// above, because atomicAdd/atomicCas only ever touch one word at a time;
// an array needs real mutual exclusion, not a single atomic op.
//
// --- haltCore() is NOT used for the completion wait below, and that's a
// deliberate choice, not an oversight ---
// The obvious design would have each worker raiseInterrupt() once done and
// have core 0 haltCore() instead of spinning. It doesn't work: now that
// every core polls the SAME shared pending-interrupt queue (see
// Bl0jv2_jVM's own per-core polling), a raise meant to wake core 0
// specifically can just as easily be drained by whichever busy worker
// happens to poll next (taskCompute's own tight loop polls constantly) -
// haltCore() only unblocks on hasPending(), so if every raise keeps
// getting stolen before core 0's own check observes it, core 0 can stay
// asleep forever even after Kernel.doneCounterAddr has already reached its
// target. This VM has no notion of a targeted, core-specific wakeup
// (a real inter-processor interrupt) - every vector is one shared table,
// fine for a device IRQ nobody cares which core services, wrong for "wake
// up THIS core". So: the counter below (already race-free, via atomicAdd)
// is polled directly instead - correct, just not as idle as it could be
// with real IPI support, which is future VM work, not something to fake
// here.
//
// --- privilege rings ---
// Core 0 (the scheduler) stays kernel-mode for its entire life - it's the
// only code in this file allowed to touch hardware directly. Every worker
// core (1..Kernel.numCores-1) is a dedicated USER core instead: the first
// task ever dispatched to it calls ensureUserMode(), which drops that
// core to user mode once and for all (there is no way back up except for
// the duration of a syscall() trap - see dropToUserMode()'s own doc).
// Every task dispatched to that worker afterward - this one or any other -
// runs unprivileged, proven by taskRogue below, which tries to bypass the
// console device's syscall gate and gets a privilege fault instead.
//
// A "process" here is really just "whichever worker core a task happens to
// land on" - simplified, but a legitimate reading of the primitives this
// VM actually provides: one ring bit per core (see CoreContext.privileged),
// not a full per-task address space/context switch, which is real future
// work, not something bolted on here.
//
// Known language limitations hit writing this (same ones from boot.bl0,
// still true): a plain 'def' function can't close over a top-level
// variable (only a lambda captures free variables) - every piece of shared
// state here is a class's static field instead, reached by explicit
// qualified name from any function regardless of nesting.

import '../stdlib/mathlib.bl0';
import '../stdlib/arrlib.bl0';
import 'kernel.bl0';
import 'lib/loader.bl0';

def class Kernel {
    static field numCores;
    static field doneCounterAddr;
    static field doneEvent;     // signalled by every taskDone(), so the boot core sleeps instead of polling
    // portConsole/sysPutcVector live here, not as plain top-level
    // variables, for the same reason every other piece of shared state in
    // this file does: a plain 'def' function (sysPutc, taskNetwork,
    // taskRogue below) can't see an enclosing top-level variable at all -
    // only a static field, reached by its explicit qualified name, works
    // from any function regardless of nesting (see this file's own header
    // comment on this limitation)
    static field portConsole;
    static field sysPutcVector;
}
Kernel.numCores = 4; // core 0 (scheduler) + 3 workers
Kernel.portConsole = 0;
Kernel.sysPutcVector = 1;

// --- kernel heap: a minimal bump allocator, same idea as boot.bl0's (see
// its own comment) - the VM provides no raw-memory allocator of its own,
// on purpose. Lock-free: atomicAdd() both returns the previous counter
// value (the address to hand out) and advances it, indivisibly, so
// concurrent kalloc() calls from different cores never race the way two
// plain peek()+poke() calls would (see Bl0jv2_AtomicsTest's own race test
// in the VM's test suite for a from-first-principles proof of that).
// counterAddr is a static field, not a top-level variable, for the same
// reason as everywhere else in this file: kalloc() is a plain 'def'
// function and can't see an enclosing top-level variable at all ---
def class HeapAlloc {
    static field counterAddr;
}
HeapAlloc.counterAddr = 0; // one 32-bit word: HeapAlloc's own bump pointer

// --- raw memory layout: two atomically-updated counters, reserved so
// kalloc() below can never hand either address out for anything else -
// the heap proper starts right after both, at HEAP_BASE ---
Kernel.doneCounterAddr = 4; // one 32-bit word, incremented by taskDone()
HEAP_BASE = 8;
reserve(0, 8);
poke32(HeapAlloc.counterAddr, HEAP_BASE);
poke32(Kernel.doneCounterAddr, 0);
Kernel.doneEvent = newEvent();

def kalloc(size) {
    return atomicAdd(HeapAlloc.counterAddr, size);
}

def kfree(addr) {
    // no-op - see boot.bl0's own HeapAlloc comment
}

// --- the console "device": a single port that only kernel code may write
// to directly (out8()/in8() are privileged instructions, same as
// reserve()/registerHandler()/dispatch() - see requirePrivileged's own
// doc). This reference VM has no real device behind a port (see PortIO's
// own doc), so the driver below also mirrors what it wrote via println,
// purely so the demo's output is observable - a real backend would drive
// actual hardware from here instead, the user-facing contract (go through
// the syscall, never touch the port yourself) wouldn't change. Both
// Kernel.portConsole and Kernel.sysPutcVector are effectively constants
// (assigned once, above, and never reassigned) - they still have to be
// static fields, not top-level variables, purely so sysPutc()/taskNetwork()/
// taskRogue() below can see them at all - see Kernel's own field comment ---

// kernel-side driver - runs ELEVATED (syscall()'s trap gate forces kernel
// mode for exactly this handler's own body, see invokeAsTrap), regardless
// of which ring the calling task was in, then the caller's own privilege
// is restored automatically once this returns
def sysPutc(byteValue) {
    out8(Kernel.portConsole, byteValue);
    println '  [console] driver wrote 0x' + Math.toHex(byteValue) + ' to port ' + Kernel.portConsole;
}

// --- syscall ABI: registers sysPutc() on the same 0-255 vector table
// registerHandler()/raiseInterrupt() use for hardware IRQs (see
// InterruptController.handlerFor's own doc) - syscall() just delivers
// synchronously instead of on the next poll ---
registerHandler(sysPutc, Kernel.sysPutcVector, 1);

// every user task calls this first. Safe to call from every task
// dispatched to a given worker, not just the first one: the first call
// actually drops privilege, every later call on that (already-unprivileged)
// core just observes isPrivileged() == false and skips - dropToUserMode()
// itself throws if called while already unprivileged (see its own doc), so
// this check is required, not optional
def ensureUserMode() {
    if (isPrivileged()) {
        dropToUserMode();
    }
}

def class Scheduler {
    static field tasks;
    static field queueLock;
    static field nextCore;
}
Scheduler.tasks = [];
Scheduler.queueLock = newMutex();
Scheduler.nextCore = 1;

def class Task {
    field name;
    field priority;
    field fn; // a plain function value - called through a local var, not
              // task.fn(), since dot-call syntax always means method
              // lookup in bl0jv2, never "call the value stored in this field"

    def init(name, priority, fn) {
        this.name = name;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.fn = fn;
    }
}

def addTask(t) {
    disableInterrupts();
    lock(Scheduler.queueLock);
    push(Scheduler.tasks, t);
    unlock(Scheduler.queueLock);
    enableInterrupts();
}

// index of the highest-priority queued task, or -1 if empty - caller must
// already hold Scheduler.queueLock
def pickIndex() {
    if (len(Scheduler.tasks) == 0) { return -1; }
    best = 0;
    i = 1;
    while (i < len(Scheduler.tasks)) {
        if (Scheduler.tasks[i].priority > Scheduler.tasks[best].priority) {
            best = i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return best;
}

// simple round-robin placement across the worker cores - the "smart"
// choice (load balancing, affinity, whatever) is exactly the kind of
// policy decision that belongs here in bl0jv2, not baked into the VM;
// round-robin is just the simplest policy that demonstrates the mechanism
def nextWorkerCore() {
    c = Scheduler.nextCore;
    Scheduler.nextCore = Scheduler.nextCore + 1;
    if (Scheduler.nextCore >= Kernel.numCores) { Scheduler.nextCore = 1; }
    return c;
}

// pops the highest-priority ready task and dispatches it onto the next
// worker core - fire-and-forget, genuinely concurrent with whatever core 0
// (the scheduler itself) does next
def dispatchNext() {
    disableInterrupts();
    lock(Scheduler.queueLock);
    idx = pickIndex();
    if (idx < 0) {
        unlock(Scheduler.queueLock);
        enableInterrupts();
        return nil;
    }
    t = Scheduler.tasks[idx];
    Arr.removeAt(Scheduler.tasks, idx); // swaps the last task in; queue order is irrelevant (pickIndex rescans by priority)
    unlock(Scheduler.queueLock);
    enableInterrupts();

    core = nextWorkerCore();
    fn = t.fn;
    println '  [scheduler] dispatching ' + t.name + ' -> core ' + core;
    dispatch(fn, core, 0);
    return nil;
}

def taskDone() {
    atomicAdd(Kernel.doneCounterAddr, 1);
    signalEvent(Kernel.doneEvent);
}

// --- demo tasks: each reports which real core it actually ran on, to
// make the parallelism observable rather than taken on faith. Every one
// calls ensureUserMode() first - the core it lands on runs the rest of
// the task, and every task dispatched to it afterward, unprivileged ---

def taskLogger(arg) {
    ensureUserMode();
    println '    [logger] running on core ' + currentCore();
    taskDone();
}

def taskNetwork(arg) {
    ensureUserMode();
    buf = kalloc(4);
    poke32(buf, 0xC0FFEE);
    println '    [network] running on core ' + currentCore() + ', packet ' + Math.toHex(peek32(buf));
    // out8() itself is privileged and this task is not - syscall() is the
    // only path left to the console device, handled by sysPutc() above.
    // buf+3: the packet's low byte (0xC0FFEE is big-endian, buf+0 is 0x00)
    syscall(Kernel.sysPutcVector, peek8(buf + 3));
    kfree(buf);
    taskDone();
}

// enough real work that genuine cross-core overlap is plausible, not just
// three near-instant tasks that happen to interleave trivially
def taskCompute(arg) {
    ensureUserMode();
    i = 0;
    sum = 0;
    while (i < 200000) { sum = sum + i; i = i + 1; }
    println '    [compute] running on core ' + currentCore() + ', sum=' + sum;
    taskDone();
}

// deliberately tries to bypass the syscall gate and talk to the console
// device directly, to prove the ring boundary is real and not just
// documented - out8() must throw here. Catches its own fault rather than
// letting it escape uncaught: unlike boot.bl0's single-core scheduler
// (whose runOnce() wraps every task in its own try/catch), dispatch() here
// has no scheduler-level fault isolation - an uncaught exception in a
// dispatched task is only logged to stderr by the worker (see CoreWorker's
// own catch block) and never calls taskDone(), which would strand the
// completion count below and hang the final wait forever
def taskRogue(arg) {
    ensureUserMode();
    try {
        out8(Kernel.portConsole, 99);
        println '    [rogue] this should never print';
    } catch (e) {
        println '    [rogue] blocked: ' + e;
    }
    taskDone();
}

println '=== aeon-os (SMP) booting: ' + Kernel.numCores + ' cores ===';

addTask(new Task('logger', 5, taskLogger));
addTask(new Task('network', 20, taskNetwork));
addTask(new Task('compute-a', 10, taskCompute));
addTask(new Task('compute-b', 10, taskCompute));
addTask(new Task('compute-c', 10, taskCompute));
addTask(new Task('rogue', 15, taskRogue));

println '';
println '--- dispatching all ready tasks across cores ---';
while (true) {
    lock(Scheduler.queueLock); remaining = len(Scheduler.tasks); unlock(Scheduler.queueLock);
    if (remaining == 0) { break; }
    dispatchNext();
}

println '';
println '--- waiting for all dispatched tasks to complete ---';
// sleeps on Kernel.doneEvent, which every task signals as it finishes - no
// polling. The generation is read BEFORE the counter is checked, so a task
// that finishes between the check and the wait still ends the wait at once
// (see waitEvent's own doc); the 100 ms cap is only a safety net.
while (true) {
    gen = eventGen(Kernel.doneEvent);
    if (peek32(Kernel.doneCounterAddr) >= 6) { break; }
    waitEvent(Kernel.doneEvent, gen, 100);
}

println '';
println 'all ' + peek32(Kernel.doneCounterAddr) + ' tasks completed across ' + (Kernel.numCores - 1) + ' worker cores';

// --- launching the interactive shell: exec()'d on core 0 itself (the only
// core that stayed privileged this whole time - see this file's own header
// comment on privilege rings), same journalExec()/kernel.bl0 pattern
// boot.bl0 uses. Its heap counter at raw address 0 is this file's own
// HeapAlloc.counterAddr - literally the same word, not a coincidence: both
// files agreed on address 0 for a bump counter, so shell.bl0's own
// import (kernel.bl0's kalloc()) just keeps advancing the counter this
// file already started, and its own peek32(0)==0 self-init check correctly
// sees it's non-zero and skips re-initializing. vector 1 is reused too -
// this file's own sysPutc (registered above, for taskNetwork's syscall
// demo) gets overwritten by kernel.bl0's initConsole() below (registerHandler
// always replaces whatever was on a vector - see InterruptController's own
// doc); harmless, since every task that needed vector 1 already ran and
// completed before the shell is launched. Run with `-e -k` to actually type
// into it (see Bl0jv2_CLI's own -k doc) - without -k this just hangs at the
// prompt like launching an interactive shell with no controlling terminal
// would on a real system ---
println '';
println '--- launching shell ---';
Disk.init(kalloc(512));
if (!Fs.mount()) {
    println 'no boot disk: start with --disk <image> holding sbin/shell.bl0c (see aeon-os/README.md)';
    panic('no boot disk');
}
journalExec('shell', 'sbin/shell.bl0c');

println '';
printJournal();

println '';
println '=== aeon-os (SMP) halted cleanly ===';
